WEBVTT

00:00:04.839 --> 00:00:10.789
<v Callum Reid>Morning, everyone. Monday the twenty-eighth. Can you all hear me, or am I talking to myself again?</v>

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:18.300
<v Hannah Price>We can hear you. I'm more concerned about the broken coffee machine in the canteen. That's a grim start.</v>

00:00:20.739 --> 00:00:24.239
<v David Whitfield>That coffee was grim when it worked, to be fair.</v>

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:32.570
<v Hannah Price>Yeah, and the heating in the portakabin's either tropical or off. Just having a moan, before that goes on your action list.</v>

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:41.369
<v Fiona Doyle>There's a birthday cake in the office, if that improves things. No idea whose birthday, but it's there.</v>

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.600
<v Marcus Hale>Good enough provenance for me.</v>

00:00:45.219 --> 00:00:48.719
<v Callum Reid>Right, before we all disappear for cake, what's already cleared?</v>

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:56.609
<v Marcus Hale>I finished the repair on the line 3 barrel last night. That's done, not carrying anything over on that.</v>

00:00:58.909 --> 00:01:01.710
<v Callum Reid>Lovely. Keeping that in the completed bit, then.</v>

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:11.290
<v Marcus Hale>And we were going to replace the belt on the line three but that's been pushed to next month so ignore it. Not happening today.</v>

00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:17.370
<v Fiona Doyle>Yeah, that's off today's schedule. The old entry isn't another job.</v>

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:25.060
<v Fiona Doyle>While we're on completed stuff, I already swapped the filter on the hot bearing yesterday.</v>

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.329
<v Grace Liu>Right, filter swap's finished. Useful to keep that separate from the bearing work.</v>

00:01:33.709 --> 00:01:42.109
<v Callum Reid>I noticed the pop valve was running a bit warm on nights but it settled down. Just an observation, nothing I'm allocating off that.</v>

00:01:44.099 --> 00:01:47.250
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, understood. Settled down, rather than an ongoing fault.</v>

00:01:48.599 --> 00:01:55.250
<v Fiona Doyle>The other thing is we can't get to the HV board until production gives us a window. Still blocked.</v>

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:00.530
<v Callum Reid>Understood. It stays blocked; we're not giving anyone that work in this meeting.</v>

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:08.860
<v Priya Raman>And the supplier still hasn't confirmed the delivery for the hot bearing. Same position as before, unfortunately.</v>

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:15.219
<v David Whitfield>That's the incoming replacement, yeah? Not saying we can't do anything on what's installed.</v>

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.150
<v Priya Raman>Yeah, exactly. Delivery's the blocker. I'm not announcing a replacement job.</v>

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.780
<v Callum Reid>PR, can you do the lock-out tag-out sign-off on the door switch on two this morning? That one's safety critical, straight away.</v>

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.810
<v Priya Raman>Yep, I'll do the lock-out tag-out sign-off on the door switch on two straight away this morning.</v>

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.159
<v Grace Liu>Yeah, the sign-off, not just somebody saying the switch looks all right.</v>

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.159
<v Priya Raman>Absolutely. Proper isolation sign-off is what I'm accepting.</v>

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.120
<v Callum Reid>Haley, can you recalibrate the sensors on the little water valve on two? Top priority please.</v>

00:02:54.409 --> 00:03:00.360
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, I'll recalibrate the sensors on the little water valve on two. Got it as top priority.</v>

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:06.689
<v Priya Raman>Sorry, talking over you there—sensors, rather than changing anything on the valve itself?</v>

00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:12.009
<v Marcus Hale>That's it, calibration on the sensors. Not a valve replacement.</v>

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:17.669
<v Callum Reid>Fiona, can you take vibration readings on the turbine two next week? It's urgent.</v>

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:23.639
<v Fiona Doyle>Me taking the readings, rather than just putting someone else in the planner?</v>

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:30.580
<v Callum Reid>Yes, you taking them, please. Next week is the timing, but it's urgent, not a spare-time one.</v>

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:37.129
<v Fiona Doyle>Right, understood. I'll take vibration readings on the turbine two next week and keep it marked urgent.</v>

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:43.150
<v David Whitfield>Easy to lose that distinction when everything on the sheet says 'arrange', isn't it.</v>

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:48.060
<v Fiona Doyle>Exactly. Otherwise I come away thinking I've only got a scheduling job.</v>

00:03:48.479 --> 00:03:54.430
<v Callum Reid>Whitty, can you update the risk assessment for work on the hot bearing for Monday? It's urgent.</v>

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:02.199
<v David Whitfield>Monday as in today, yeah? Just making sure I'm not mentally sticking it a week out.</v>

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:04.830
<v Callum Reid>Yeah, Monday, today. It's urgent.</v>

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:12.169
<v David Whitfield>Okay, I'll update the risk assessment for work on the hot bearing for Monday. Understood on the urgency.</v>

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:23.069
<v Grace Liu>And I'll grease and check the bearings on the hot bearing at half two. Not an emergency but don't let it slip—that's how I've got that one.</v>

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:29.850
<v Callum Reid>Thanks, Grace. Agreed, at half two, and that priority's right: not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:04:31.829 --> 00:04:36.379
<v Grace Liu>Yep. Grease and bearing checks, separate from the filter that's already been swapped.</v>

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:47.699
<v Hannah Price>I'll organise the toolbox talk for the team at half two as well. That's a whenever you get a chance one, rather than something to drop everything for.</v>

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.689
<v Callum Reid>Cheers, Han. At half two for organising that, then. Yeah, whenever you get a chance in terms of priority.</v>

00:04:57.819 --> 00:05:04.470
<v Hannah Price>Yep, that's what I mean. I'll organise it; I'm not trying to pull everyone off the floor right now.</v>

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:12.310
<v Grace Liu>I heard half two twice and thought I'd volunteered to be in two places. Different jobs, different people. Fine.</v>

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.560
<v Callum Reid>Yeah, no double booking hidden in that.</v>

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:23.259
<v Callum Reid>Fiona Doyle, can you run the monthly fire alarm test as well? Fairly important, that one.</v>

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.019
<v Fiona Doyle>Yes, I'll run the monthly fire alarm test. Got that as fairly important.</v>

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:34.839
<v Hannah Price>That's separate from my toolbox talk, just so nobody's bundled them together.</v>

00:05:35.839 --> 00:05:40.040
<v Callum Reid>Yep, two separate things. Not making the talk into an alarm test.</v>

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.980
<v Fiona Doyle>And I'll send the shift handover report round next Tuesday.</v>

00:05:47.769 --> 00:05:50.920
<v Callum Reid>Thanks, Doyler. Shift handover report round next Tuesday, agreed.</v>

00:05:52.209 --> 00:05:57.459
<v Fiona Doyle>Yep, next Tuesday. That's the report circulation, not another bit of work on the equipment.</v>

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:04.490
<v Marcus Hale>I'll check the guarding on the palletiser as well. Not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.970
<v Callum Reid>Cheers, Marc. Guarding on the palletiser is yours, with that priority.</v>

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:15.050
<v Marcus Hale>Yep, I'll do the guarding check. That's what I'm taking on.</v>

00:06:17.439 --> 00:06:22.339
<v David Whitfield>Not the belt job sneaking back onto the list under a different name, then.</v>

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:29.779
<v Marcus Hale>No, definitely not. The belt on the line three is still pushed to next month. Different thing entirely.</v>

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:36.870
<v Callum Reid>Right. And the HV board access and the unconfirmed delivery stay as blockers, not fresh actions we've handed out.</v>

00:06:39.129 --> 00:06:42.629
<v Fiona Doyle>Correct. Neither's suddenly become available because we've talked about it.</v>

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:48.350
<v Priya Raman>If only Teams worked like that. Half our problems would disappear on mute.</v>

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:55.029
<v Hannah Price>Unlike the coffee machine. Still broken when I unmute. Cake might have to do.</v>

00:06:55.759 --> 00:07:00.660
<v Callum Reid>Right, that's the lot. Thanks, all. Clear enough despite us talking over each other.</v>

00:07:02.209 --> 00:07:03.610
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, all clear. Cheers.</v>
